WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.955 --> 00:00:05.969
Priya Lindqvist: Hi everyone, welcome to section.

2
00:00:06.144 --> 00:00:11.076
Priya Lindqvist: Let's give it one more minute
for people coming from the other building. Okay.

3
00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:17.211
Priya Lindqvist: So this is Section 6, and
today is all about Problem Set 5 and Milestone

4
00:00:17.674 --> 00:00:23.003
Priya Lindqvist: 2, because, uh, they're due two
days apart and they use the same ideas.

5
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:27.562
Priya Lindqvist: We'll do tombstones one more
time, then resizing, and then I want

6
00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:32.241
Priya Lindqvist: to spend real time on
how to benchmark your key-value store, because

7
00:00:32.454 --> 00:00:36.677
Priya Lindqvist: last year that's where teams
lost the most points on the milestone.

8
00:00:37.072 --> 00:00:42.022
Priya Lindqvist: Bring up questions whenever, this
is section, it's supposed to be loose.

9
00:00:42.349 --> 00:00:48.533
Priya Lindqvist: Quick poll, who's started PS5? Okay,
about half. Who's started the milestone? Fewer hands.

10
00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:50.987
Priya Lindqvist: That's fine, that's why we're here.

11
00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.914
Priya Lindqvist: Also, the room after us has a lab, so

12
00:00:55.389 --> 00:00:58.243
Priya Lindqvist: we have to be out at 4:50 sharp.

13
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.067
Priya Lindqvist: Okay. Tombstones, one more time, because
this is the part of PS5 people get wrong.

14
00:01:07.221 --> 00:01:11.964
Priya Lindqvist: This is the same
little table from Thursday's lecture, 8 slots.

15
00:01:12.109 --> 00:01:16.354
Priya Lindqvist: Fig was in slot
5, and we deleted it, so

16
00:01:16.639 --> 00:01:20.706
Priya Lindqvist: now slot 5 holds
a tombstone instead of being empty.

17
00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:26.315
Priya Lindqvist: Lime had collided with fig, so
it lives in slot 6. So, look up lime.

18
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:30.922
Priya Lindqvist: You hash to 5, you
see the tombstone, and you keep going,

19
00:01:31.113 --> 00:01:35.185
Priya Lindqvist: because a tombstone means
something used to be here, keep probing.

20
00:01:35.481 --> 00:01:38.132
Priya Lindqvist: Then you find lime in slot 6.

21
00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:43.017
Priya Lindqvist: If slot 5 were
just empty, you'd stop and say lime

22
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:47.893
Priya Lindqvist: isn't in the table,
which is exactly the bug from lecture.

23
00:01:48.373 --> 00:01:53.824
Priya Lindqvist: Draw this on paper for
your own table before you write any code.

24
00:01:54.315 --> 00:01:59.178
Priya Lindqvist: Seriously, five minutes with a
pencil saves an hour of print statements.

25
00:01:59.938 --> 00:02:05.464
Student: What happens if you insert
a new key that hashes to 5?

26
00:02:06.661 --> 00:02:12.131
Priya Lindqvist: You can reuse the tombstone slot,
but only after you've checked the rest of the

27
00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:17.420
Priya Lindqvist: probe chain to make sure the
key isn't already in the table further along.

28
00:02:17.897 --> 00:02:22.028
Priya Lindqvist: Otherwise you end up
with the same key in the

29
00:02:22.266 --> 00:02:26.597
Priya Lindqvist: table twice, and that's
a really annoying bug to find.

30
00:02:30.005 --> 00:02:32.009
Priya Lindqvist: Next. When do you resize?

31
00:02:32.212 --> 00:02:37.163
Priya Lindqvist: You track the load factor, alpha,
which is n over m, after every insert.

32
00:02:37.587 --> 00:02:41.398
Priya Lindqvist: When it passes your
threshold, you double m and rehash

33
00:02:41.765 --> 00:02:45.647
Priya Lindqvist: every key, because every key's slot depends on m.

34
00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.765
Priya Lindqvist: And, oh, wait, that third bullet is wrong. Sorry.

35
00:02:50.104 --> 00:02:54.476
Priya Lindqvist: I mixed up my
languages when I made this slide.

36
00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:58.900
Priya Lindqvist: CPython's dict resizes at
about two-thirds full, not three quarters.

37
00:02:59.173 --> 00:03:02.347
Priya Lindqvist: Three quarters, 0.75, is the default for Java's HashMap.

38
00:03:02.645 --> 00:03:07.426
Priya Lindqvist: Professor Vasquez-Hale said this in
lecture, so don't let my slide confuse you.

39
00:03:07.551 --> 00:03:11.895
Priya Lindqvist: For PS5, the threshold
is given to you in the starter

40
00:03:12.205 --> 00:03:16.357
Priya Lindqvist: code, it's 0.7, so
you don't have to pick one.

41
00:03:16.834 --> 00:03:22.032
Priya Lindqvist: You can play with it in
your own copy, but the tests assume 0.7.

42
00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:26.899
Student: Why do we double the table instead of

43
00:03:27.123 --> 00:03:30.848
Student: just adding, like, a hundred slots each time?

44
00:03:31.979 --> 00:03:34.836
Priya Lindqvist: Because doubling is what makes inserts amortized O(1).

45
00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.817
Priya Lindqvist: If you add a
fixed hundred slots, you resize every

46
00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:43.477
Priya Lindqvist: hundred inserts, and the
total copying grows like n squared.

47
00:03:43.792 --> 00:03:50.024
Priya Lindqvist: With doubling, the total copying stays
under 2n. Same argument as dynamic arrays on PS4.

48
00:03:50.448 --> 00:03:53.666
Priya Lindqvist: It's also why Python lists grow by a fraction

49
00:03:54.077 --> 00:03:57.250
Priya Lindqvist: of their size, not by a fixed amount.

50
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:03.242
Priya Lindqvist: Okay, now the one
that bites people every single year.

51
00:04:03.562 --> 00:04:09.559
Priya Lindqvist: When you decide whether to resize,
count tombstones as occupied slots, not just live keys.

52
00:04:09.891 --> 00:04:10.979
Priya Lindqvist: Think about it.

53
00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:17.250
Priya Lindqvist: If you only count live keys,
a table that does a lot of deletes

54
00:04:17.744 --> 00:04:23.732
Priya Lindqvist: never looks full, so it never
resizes, and it slowly fills up with tombstones.

55
00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:28.404
Priya Lindqvist: And then every lookup for
a missing key walks the whole table.

56
00:04:28.822 --> 00:04:32.677
Priya Lindqvist: There's a hidden test
on PS5 that does exactly this.

57
00:04:33.019 --> 00:04:36.216
Priya Lindqvist: It inserts and deletes 10,000 times in a loop,

58
00:04:36.402 --> 00:04:40.154
Priya Lindqvist: and if you don't count tombstones, it times out.

59
00:04:40.505 --> 00:04:45.237
Priya Lindqvist: So, count them. And
the good news is on the slide.

60
00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:51.025
Priya Lindqvist: When you do resize, the tombstones
disappear, because you only reinsert the live keys.

61
00:04:51.746 --> 00:04:57.369
Student: So for the experiment part, should
the load factor we report include tombstones too?

62
00:04:58.612 --> 00:05:01.568
Priya Lindqvist: For deciding when to resize, yes, count them.

63
00:05:01.859 --> 00:05:07.758
Priya Lindqvist: For the experiment, report live keys
over slots, and say so in your write-up.

64
00:05:08.114 --> 00:05:11.141
Priya Lindqvist: Just be explicit about which one you mean.

65
00:05:13.144 --> 00:05:17.835
Priya Lindqvist: This is the picture I
want you to keep in your head.

66
00:05:18.246 --> 00:05:23.481
Priya Lindqvist: It's the expected number of
probes for a lookup that misses, as

67
00:05:23.738 --> 00:05:27.631
Priya Lindqvist: the load factor goes
up, for chaining and for linear probing.

68
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:31.140
Priya Lindqvist: These are the textbook estimates, not our measurements.

69
00:05:31.519 --> 00:05:35.468
Priya Lindqvist: Look at how flat
one line stays, and then look

70
00:05:35.675 --> 00:05:39.229
Priya Lindqvist: at what the other
one does at the right edge.

71
00:05:39.376 --> 00:05:43.912
Priya Lindqvist: That's the whole reason we
resize long before the table is full.

72
00:05:44.084 --> 00:05:47.682
Priya Lindqvist: On PS5 you measure
this yourself for linear probing, so

73
00:05:48.057 --> 00:05:52.056
Priya Lindqvist: you should get a
curve shaped like one of these.

74
00:05:53.172 --> 00:05:57.839
Student: Why does linear probing
get so much worse than chaining?

75
00:05:58.556 --> 00:05:59.277
Priya Lindqvist: Primary clustering.

76
00:05:59.557 --> 00:06:04.289
Priya Lindqvist: Runs of filled slots grow
and merge, so a miss has to

77
00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:08.786
Priya Lindqvist: walk all the way
to the end of a long run.

78
00:06:09.152 --> 00:06:12.354
Priya Lindqvist: With chaining, a miss only walks one bucket's list.

79
00:06:12.833 --> 00:06:16.431
Priya Lindqvist: It's also roughly what your PS5 experiment should show.

80
00:06:16.925 --> 00:06:21.211
Priya Lindqvist: If your measured curve
doesn't bend up like this one,

81
00:06:21.475 --> 00:06:24.638
Priya Lindqvist: something is off, and it's usually your hash function.

82
00:06:27.491 --> 00:06:28.701
Priya Lindqvist: Okay, Milestone 2.

83
00:06:29.197 --> 00:06:32.389
Priya Lindqvist: Your write-up has to compare your store against a

84
00:06:32.862 --> 00:06:36.841
Priya Lindqvist: plain Python dict for at least two workload sizes.

85
00:06:37.138 --> 00:06:40.953
Priya Lindqvist: Three rules on the slide. Use time.perf_counter, not time.time.

86
00:06:41.361 --> 00:06:44.702
Priya Lindqvist: Repeat each measurement 5 times and report the median.

87
00:06:44.939 --> 00:06:47.863
Priya Lindqvist: And time the operations, not the setup, so

88
00:06:48.318 --> 00:06:51.710
Priya Lindqvist: build your keys before you start the timer.

89
00:06:51.833 --> 00:06:53.131
Priya Lindqvist: Here's a story.

90
00:06:53.625 --> 00:06:58.930
Priya Lindqvist: Last year, one team reported that
their table was 40 times slower than dict.

91
00:06:59.078 --> 00:07:03.582
Priya Lindqvist: It turned out they were
generating random strings inside the timed loop,

92
00:07:03.872 --> 00:07:08.230
Priya Lindqvist: so most of what
they measured was the random number generator.

93
00:07:08.443 --> 00:07:13.185
Priya Lindqvist: And honestly, you will be
slower than dict. Dict is written in C.

94
00:07:13.524 --> 00:07:17.795
Priya Lindqvist: If you're within 10 times
of dict at a million keys, that's

95
00:07:18.092 --> 00:07:22.933
Priya Lindqvist: a good result, and nobody
loses points for being slower than dict.

96
00:07:23.078 --> 00:07:28.795
Priya Lindqvist: Also, run the benchmark on a
quiet machine. Close the browser with a hundred tabs.

97
00:07:29.131 --> 00:07:33.851
Priya Lindqvist: And put the machine
details in the write-up, the CPU and

98
00:07:34.234 --> 00:07:39.068
Priya Lindqvist: the Python version, so
we can make sense of your numbers.

99
00:07:40.303 --> 00:07:43.473
Student: Do we have to benchmark delete too?

100
00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.573
Priya Lindqvist: Get and put are required in the benchmark.

101
00:07:47.714 --> 00:07:52.320
Priya Lindqvist: Delete has to work in
the prototype, but benchmarking it is optional.

102
00:07:52.777 --> 00:07:56.281
Priya Lindqvist: If you do benchmark
it, run it after a big

103
00:07:56.484 --> 00:07:59.772
Priya Lindqvist: batch of inserts, so the tombstones actually show up.

104
00:08:03.001 --> 00:08:07.580
Priya Lindqvist: Here's the checklist, same
as the email I sent this morning.

105
00:08:07.833 --> 00:08:12.759
Priya Lindqvist: A repo link with a
working get, put and delete, no persistence yet.

106
00:08:12.933 --> 00:08:19.260
Priya Lindqvist: The two-page write-up with the benchmark.
And one paragraph on how you split the work.

107
00:08:19.742 --> 00:08:22.922
Priya Lindqvist: If someone on your team has been quiet, the

108
00:08:23.339 --> 00:08:26.756
Priya Lindqvist: split paragraph is where we notice, so be honest.

109
00:08:26.909 --> 00:08:30.141
Priya Lindqvist: It's not graded on how even the split is.

110
00:08:30.613 --> 00:08:36.139
Priya Lindqvist: It's due Sunday, November 8th at
11:59 PM, and only one person per team submits.

111
00:08:39.639 --> 00:08:44.875
Priya Lindqvist: Last slide. PS5 is due Friday
at 5 PM, code and experiment write-up together.

112
00:08:45.256 --> 00:08:49.267
Priya Lindqvist: And one new thing that's not on the slide.

113
00:08:49.476 --> 00:08:53.279
Priya Lindqvist: The autograder for PS5 opens this Wednesday at noon.

114
00:08:53.404 --> 00:08:57.562
Priya Lindqvist: You get 10 submissions
a day, so please don't use

115
00:08:57.953 --> 00:09:01.362
Priya Lindqvist: it as your debugger, run the local tests first.

116
00:09:01.621 --> 00:09:04.775
Priya Lindqvist: And if you use a late day, you don't

117
00:09:05.242 --> 00:09:08.114
Priya Lindqvist: need to email anyone, the portal tracks it.

118
00:09:08.528 --> 00:09:09.707
Priya Lindqvist: Okay, that's it.

119
00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:15.442
Priya Lindqvist: Go get some sleep, and, um,
